    "pull_request": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/tensorflow/tensorflow/pulls/110502",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/tensorflow/tensorflow/pull/110502",
      "diff_url": "https://github.com/tensorflow/tensorflow/pull/110502.diff",
      "patch_url": "https://github.com/tensorflow/tensorflow/pull/110502.patch",
      "merged_at": null
    },
    "body": "Current shared libraries for python packages export excessive amounts of global symbols which can conflct with other libraries (see e.g. https://github.com/tensorflow/tensorflow/issues/104038)\r\n\r\nThis is a significant step towards addressing this by\r\n1) building a single monolithic .so file for _pywrap_tensorflow (tensorflow_cc and tensorflow_framework still exist as mostly empty stubs)\r\n2) enabling wrap_py_init by default for all python extensions so that the individual .so files are mostly empty and depend ONLY on Wrapped_PyInit_* symbols from the common .so file\r\n3) Maximally restricting global symbols for the remaining .so file from  _pywrap_tensorflow to just Wrapped_PyInit_* plus whatever is needed to support custom op registration\r\n\r\nThe set of symbols exported to support custom op registration can possibly be further reduced (and ideally a better setup put in place to mitigate the need for this and at least restrict to a specific namespace.\r\n\r\nIn case anyone is curious the specific use case we are trying to address here is when using the tensorflow python package in conjunction with ROOT/cling/cppyy with jitted code including Eigen headers where there are otherwise many potentially conflicting symbols (and a few problematic cases in practice).  See e.g. https://github.com/bendavid/narf/blob/6a6fc83aa04b4d44f44f4dd9fb269af57948a4a2/narf/graph_builder.py#L13-L36 for the kind of grief this causes.  But the symbol visibility issue is clearly much more generic.\r\n",
    "closed_by": null,
